1
00:00:00,116 --> 00:00:02,984
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:03,320 --> 00:00:07,175
In today's T-Level Chemistry lesson, we are going to study

3
00:00:07,384 --> 00:00:11,622
A eleven point two, Temperature and Rate of Reaction.

4
00:00:12,016 --> 00:00:15,406
By the end of this lesson, you should be able to explain

5
00:00:15,488 --> 00:00:19,470
why increasing temperature increases particle kinetic

6
00:00:19,563 --> 00:00:23,243
energy and speed, how that changes collision frequency

7
00:00:23,417 --> 00:00:27,074
and the proportion of successful collisions, what happens when

8
00:00:27,132 --> 00:00:31,451
temperature falls, and how to read that change on a Maxwell-Boltzmann

9
00:00:31,532 --> 00:00:32,403
distribution.

10
00:00:32,682 --> 00:00:34,911
First, retrieve collision theory.

11
00:00:35,062 --> 00:00:38,603
What two conditions are required for a successful collision?

12
00:00:38,777 --> 00:00:43,119
Sufficient collision energy, and the correct spatial orientation.

13
00:00:43,270 --> 00:00:47,334
Activation energy is the minimum collision energy required

14
00:00:47,403 --> 00:00:49,215
for a successful reaction.

15
00:00:49,458 --> 00:00:52,999
If particles move faster, they collide more frequently.

16
00:00:53,174 --> 00:00:56,970
If they have more kinetic energy, a greater proportion can

17
00:00:57,051 --> 00:01:01,208
have energy equal to or greater than the activation energy.

18
00:01:01,544 --> 00:01:05,317
Specification point A eleven point two point

19
00:01:05,410 --> 00:01:05,828
one.

20
00:01:06,165 --> 00:01:09,996
Temperature is linked to the kinetic energy of particles.

21
00:01:10,333 --> 00:01:14,176
When temperature increases, particles have greater average

22
00:01:14,222 --> 00:01:17,043
kinetic energy, so they move faster.

23
00:01:17,380 --> 00:01:18,947
Watch the particle model.

24
00:01:19,284 --> 00:01:23,185
At higher temperature the particles travel more quickly through

25
00:01:23,220 --> 00:01:23,916
the mixture.

26
00:01:24,439 --> 00:01:26,970
That faster movement has two effects.

27
00:01:27,214 --> 00:01:29,129
First: collision frequency.

28
00:01:29,408 --> 00:01:33,100
Faster particles encounter other reactant particles more

29
00:01:33,146 --> 00:01:36,757
often, so there are more collisions per unit time,

30
00:01:36,907 --> 00:01:40,274
and more opportunities for successful collisions.

31
00:01:40,727 --> 00:01:44,164
Second: the proportion of successful collisions.

32
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:47,124
The particle-energy distribution changes.

33
00:01:47,461 --> 00:01:51,141
A greater proportion of particles have energy equal to or

34
00:01:51,199 --> 00:01:55,135
greater than the activation energy, so a greater proportion

35
00:01:55,205 --> 00:01:58,421
of collisions can satisfy the energy requirement.

36
00:01:59,036 --> 00:02:01,579
Correct orientation is still required.

37
00:02:01,916 --> 00:02:05,097
Increased temperature does not remove that condition.

38
00:02:05,549 --> 00:02:06,873
Put the chain together.

39
00:02:07,082 --> 00:02:11,065
Higher temperature means greater average kinetic energy.

40
00:02:11,239 --> 00:02:15,221
Particles move faster, so collisions occur more frequently.

41
00:02:15,349 --> 00:02:19,180
A greater proportion of particles have energy at least equal

42
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:20,863
to activation energy.

43
00:02:21,142 --> 00:02:25,066
Successful collisions therefore occur more frequently, and the rate

44
00:02:25,101 --> 00:02:26,540
of reaction increases.

45
00:02:26,749 --> 00:02:30,662
Now specification point A eleven point two point two:

46
00:02:30,871 --> 00:02:33,123
what happens when temperature decreases?

47
00:02:33,402 --> 00:02:36,769
Lower temperature means lower average kinetic energy.

48
00:02:36,943 --> 00:02:38,638
Particles move more slowly.

49
00:02:38,847 --> 00:02:40,786
Collision frequency decreases.

50
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:44,850
A smaller proportion of particles have energy equal to or

51
00:02:44,943 --> 00:02:46,858
greater than activation energy.

52
00:02:47,195 --> 00:02:51,015
Successful collisions occur less frequently, so reaction

53
00:02:51,107 --> 00:02:52,234
rate decreases.

54
00:02:52,350 --> 00:02:53,905
Avoid a common shortcut.

55
00:02:54,149 --> 00:02:57,945
It is incomplete to say only that particles collide

56
00:02:58,027 --> 00:02:58,479
less.

57
00:02:58,688 --> 00:03:02,485
A full explanation includes fewer collisions per unit

58
00:03:02,531 --> 00:03:06,246
time and a smaller proportion of collisions with sufficient

59
00:03:06,316 --> 00:03:06,978
energy.

60
00:03:07,129 --> 00:03:10,763
The activation energy of the same reaction has not changed

61
00:03:10,937 --> 00:03:13,247
simply because temperature changed.

62
00:03:13,421 --> 00:03:17,044
Orientation remains a requirement at all temperatures.

63
00:03:17,438 --> 00:03:21,328
Specification point A eleven point two point three.

64
00:03:21,781 --> 00:03:25,473
The Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution shows how particle energies

65
00:03:25,554 --> 00:03:29,025
are distributed in a sample at a particular temperature.

66
00:03:29,118 --> 00:03:31,951
The horizontal axis is particle energy.

67
00:03:32,067 --> 00:03:35,689
The vertical axis is the number, or fraction, of

68
00:03:35,782 --> 00:03:37,744
particles with a given energy.

69
00:03:37,988 --> 00:03:41,820
Activation energy can be shown as a vertical threshold, E

70
00:03:41,971 --> 00:03:42,331
a.

71
00:03:42,783 --> 00:03:44,711
Now compare two temperatures.

72
00:03:45,105 --> 00:03:48,728
The higher-temperature curve is broader, has a lower peak,

73
00:03:48,902 --> 00:03:51,410
and is shifted toward higher energies.

74
00:03:51,688 --> 00:03:55,601
The total number of particles is unchanged, so the total

75
00:03:55,694 --> 00:03:59,826
area under each distribution is the same for the same sample.

76
00:04:00,279 --> 00:04:03,785
The key rate comparison is the area to the right of E

77
00:04:03,855 --> 00:04:04,192
a.

78
00:04:04,645 --> 00:04:08,441
Particles to the right of the activation-energy line have

79
00:04:08,511 --> 00:04:11,158
energy greater than or equal to E a.

80
00:04:11,773 --> 00:04:15,767
At higher temperature, that area is larger, so a greater

81
00:04:15,825 --> 00:04:19,644
proportion of particles have sufficient energy for a successful

82
00:04:19,737 --> 00:04:20,364
collision.

83
00:04:20,759 --> 00:04:23,766
That helps explain the increased reaction rate.

84
00:04:23,993 --> 00:04:25,723
What changes with temperature?

85
00:04:25,932 --> 00:04:29,949
The shape of the energy distribution, the average kinetic energy,

86
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:32,863
and the proportion with energy at least E a.

87
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:36,811
What does not change for the same uncatalysed reaction?

88
00:04:37,147 --> 00:04:39,632
The activation-energy threshold itself.

89
00:04:39,969 --> 00:04:43,115
The graph supports the energy part of collision theory.

90
00:04:43,394 --> 00:04:47,097
Successful collisions still also require correct orientation.

91
00:04:47,434 --> 00:04:48,862
Guided interpretation.

92
00:04:49,106 --> 00:04:51,276
Curve A is taller and narrower.

93
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:53,564
Curve B is lower and broader.

94
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:56,257
The activation-energy line is fixed.

95
00:04:56,466 --> 00:04:58,370
Which curve is the higher temperature?

96
00:04:58,707 --> 00:04:59,438
Curve B.

97
00:04:59,612 --> 00:05:03,293
It is broader and shifted toward higher energies, with a lower

98
00:05:03,362 --> 00:05:03,792
peak.

99
00:05:04,129 --> 00:05:07,669
Curve B has the larger area to the right of E a,

100
00:05:07,878 --> 00:05:11,338
so a greater proportion of particles have sufficient energy.

101
00:05:11,675 --> 00:05:15,738
Together with increased collision frequency, that gives a greater reaction

102
00:05:15,819 --> 00:05:16,203
rate.

103
00:05:16,446 --> 00:05:19,163
Correct four errors before the examination.

104
00:05:19,558 --> 00:05:23,366
Heating does not lower activation energy for the same reaction.

105
00:05:23,575 --> 00:05:26,071
It changes the particle-energy distribution.

106
00:05:26,407 --> 00:05:29,380
The peak does not get higher when temperature increases.

107
00:05:29,658 --> 00:05:33,118
The distribution becomes broader and the peak becomes lower.

108
00:05:33,571 --> 00:05:36,229
Collision frequency is not the only change.

109
00:05:36,508 --> 00:05:40,339
The proportion of particles with energy at least E a also

110
00:05:40,421 --> 00:05:41,233
increases.

111
00:05:41,477 --> 00:05:45,366
And every collision above E a is not automatically successful.

112
00:05:45,610 --> 00:05:48,118
Correct spatial orientation is still required.

113
00:05:48,571 --> 00:05:50,568
Examination-style question.

114
00:05:51,021 --> 00:05:54,666
A reaction mixture is heated from two hundred and ninety eight kelvin

115
00:05:54,748 --> 00:05:56,802
to three hundred and eighteen kelvin.

116
00:05:57,046 --> 00:05:57,696
Part a.

117
00:05:57,871 --> 00:06:01,690
Explain how the increase in temperature affects particle kinetic energy

118
00:06:01,760 --> 00:06:03,153
and collision frequency.

119
00:06:03,281 --> 00:06:04,082
Two marks.

120
00:06:04,163 --> 00:06:06,381
Average kinetic energy increases.

121
00:06:06,590 --> 00:06:09,608
Particles move faster and collide more frequently.

122
00:06:09,852 --> 00:06:10,468
Part b.

123
00:06:10,746 --> 00:06:14,485
Explain why the proportion of successful collisions increases.

124
00:06:14,694 --> 00:06:15,448
Two marks.

125
00:06:15,727 --> 00:06:19,651
A greater proportion of particles have energy equal to or greater

126
00:06:19,709 --> 00:06:23,726
than E a, so more collisions satisfy the energy requirement.

127
00:06:24,063 --> 00:06:24,806
Part c.

128
00:06:24,980 --> 00:06:28,359
Describe how the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution changes.

129
00:06:28,603 --> 00:06:29,438
Three marks.

130
00:06:29,647 --> 00:06:33,409
The distribution becomes broader, its peak becomes lower, and

131
00:06:33,479 --> 00:06:37,170
a greater area lies at higher energies, to the right of E

132
00:06:37,252 --> 00:06:37,612
a.

133
00:06:37,890 --> 00:06:38,459
Part d.

134
00:06:38,796 --> 00:06:42,639
Explain why the reaction rate increases, using collision theory.

135
00:06:42,848 --> 00:06:43,672
Three marks.

136
00:06:43,881 --> 00:06:47,619
Collision frequency increases and a greater proportion of collisions have

137
00:06:47,666 --> 00:06:51,555
sufficient energy, so successful collisions occur more frequently

138
00:06:51,625 --> 00:06:52,879
and rate increases.

139
00:06:53,053 --> 00:06:55,363
Correct orientation is still required.

140
00:06:55,607 --> 00:06:57,604
Before we finish, complete the model.

141
00:06:57,883 --> 00:07:01,981
Higher temperature increases average kinetic energy and particle speed.

142
00:07:02,155 --> 00:07:04,071
Collision frequency increases.

143
00:07:04,245 --> 00:07:08,123
The Maxwell-Boltzmann curve becomes broader and lower, with a larger

144
00:07:08,193 --> 00:07:09,609
area beyond E a.

145
00:07:09,783 --> 00:07:13,522
Successful collisions occur more frequently, so rate increases.

146
00:07:13,858 --> 00:07:15,600
Lower temperature does the reverse.

147
00:07:15,844 --> 00:07:20,185
Temperature does not, by itself, change E a for the same uncatalysed

148
00:07:20,232 --> 00:07:20,847
reaction.

149
